  data: {
    LLM: 'The game consists of 8 Phases:\n1-2. Einfacher Dreck: Standard 66 play. Players drop out as they reach 66 points in a deal.\n3. Hosen Runter: Played with open cards.\n4. Scharfer Dreck: Strict 66 with no declarations allowed.\n5. Rufer/Bettel: Players bid to win or lose a specific number of tricks.\n6-8. Special Phases: Usually includes Solo (a player plays against the rest), Wenz (only Unters are trumps), and a final elimination round.',
    Player: 'The game consists of 8 Phases:\n1-2. Einfacher Dreck: Standard 66 play. Players drop out as they reach 66 points in a deal.\n3. Hosen Runter: Played with open cards.\n4. Scharfer Dreck: Strict 66 with no declarations allowed.\n5. Rufer/Bettel: Players bid to win or lose a specific number of tricks.\n6-8. Special Phases: Usually includes Solo (a player plays against the rest), Wenz (only Unters are trumps), and a final elimination round.',
    objective: 'Avoid being the last player with strokes left on your slate at the end of each of the eight phases.',
    gameplay: 'The game consists of 8 Phases:\n1-2. Einfacher Dreck: Standard 66 play. Players drop out as they reach 66 points in a deal.\n3. Hosen Runter: Played with open cards.\n4. Scharfer Dreck: Strict 66 with no declarations allowed.\n5. Rufer/Bettel: Players bid to win or lose a specific number of tricks.\n6-8. Special Phases: Usually includes Solo (a player plays against the rest), Wenz (only Unters are trumps), and a final elimination round.',
    keyRules: [
      "The 'Last Trick' is vital for tiebreaking but does not award points like in standard 66.",
      'Marriages: Declaring King and Ober of the same suit scores 20 (or 40 if trump).',
      'Elimination: In most phases, the last person remaining (who failed to erase their strokes) is the official loser of that phase.',
